WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.561
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.861 --> 00:00:14.334
Unit twenty-one: News and Information. Today you will judge information with might and could.

4
00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:17.252
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.456
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.317 --> 00:00:23.295
News.

7
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:26.875
News means information about recent events.

8
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:29.274
I read the news every morning.

9
00:00:29.883 --> 00:00:32.791
Your turn. Say: News.

10
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:36.495
News.

11
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.216
Headline.

12
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:41.646
Headline means the title of a news story.

13
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.064
The headline was very shocking.

14
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.896
Your turn. Say: Headline.

15
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:50.688
Headline.

16
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:52.289
Source.

17
00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:55.689
Source means where information comes from.

18
00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:57.958
Always check the source.

19
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.168
Your turn. Say: Source.

20
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:05.116
Source.

21
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:06.921
Reliable.

22
00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:10.044
Reliable means able to be trusted.

23
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.487
Is this website reliable?

24
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:16.003
Your turn. Say: Reliable.

25
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:20.019
Reliable.

26
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:21.448
Fake.

27
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.508
Fake means not real or not true.

28
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:26.547
That photo is fake.

29
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.222
Your turn. Say: Fake.

30
00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:32.743
Fake.

31
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:34.194
Check.

32
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:38.216
Check means to look carefully to see if something is right.

33
00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:40.677
Check the facts before you share.

34
00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:44.179
Your turn. Say: Check.

35
00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:47.860
Check.

36
00:01:48.617 --> 00:01:49.571
Report.

37
00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:53.094
Report means a news story about an event.

38
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:55.590
The report used real numbers.

39
00:01:56.183 --> 00:01:58.627
Your turn. Say: Report.

40
00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:02.340
Report.

41
00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:04.038
Opinion.

42
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.885
Opinion means what someone thinks, not a fact.

43
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:10.483
That is an opinion, not a fact.

44
00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:13.805
Your turn. Say: Opinion.

45
00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:17.505
Opinion.

46
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.125
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:22.983 --> 00:02:26.811
Visal, did you see this headline? 'Chocolate makes you smarter!'

48
00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:30.499
Hmm. This story might be fake.

49
00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:32.944
Why do you think so?

50
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.479
There's no source. It could be true, but I need to check.

51
00:02:39.583 --> 00:02:40.770
How will you check?

52
00:02:41.883 --> 00:02:46.228
I'll look for a reliable report with facts, not just opinion.

53
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:49.239
Part three: grammar.

54
00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:53.116
Grammar focus: Modal verbs of possibility.

55
00:02:53.416 --> 00:02:54.510
Look at the pattern.

56
00:02:55.217 --> 00:02:56.752
This story might be fake.

57
00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:58.930
It could be true.

58
00:02:59.817 --> 00:03:01.421
The photo might be edited.

59
00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:07.596
Use 'might' and 'could' when you are not sure. Use 'must' or 'can't' when you are almost certain.

60
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.954
Be careful! Don't say: This story might is fake.

61
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.155
Say: This story might be fake.

62
00:03:16.455 --> 00:03:18.420
After 'might' or 'could', use 'be'.

63
00:03:19.233 --> 00:03:22.083
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:23.914
Is It True?.

65
00:03:24.214 --> 00:03:26.156
We get news from many places.

66
00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:30.165
A headline can be misleading, so check the source.

67
00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:34.792
A reliable report uses facts, not only opinion.

68
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.904
Fake news spreads quickly online.

69
00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.303
If a story seems strange, it might be fake, so think before you share.

70
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.553
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

71
00:03:49.417 --> 00:03:51.230
This story blank be fake.

72
00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.545
A, might. B, might to. C, mights.

73
00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.821
The answer is A: might.

74
00:04:00.983 --> 00:04:03.285
Where information comes from is the blank.

75
00:04:03.585 --> 00:04:07.145
A, source. B, headline. C, opinion.

76
00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.676
The answer is A: source.

77
00:04:13.817 --> 00:04:16.617
What someone thinks, not a fact, is an blank.

78
00:04:16.917 --> 00:04:20.838
A, opinion. B, report. C, source.

79
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.086
The answer is A: opinion.

80
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.544
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

81
00:04:31.383 --> 00:04:33.569
How do you decide if news is reliable?

82
00:04:42.383 --> 00:04:43.988
Now listen to Visal.

83
00:04:44.288 --> 00:04:53.129
I check the source first. If the news comes from an unknown website, it might be fake, so I compare it with a reliable news site.

84
00:04:53.429 --> 00:04:55.266
Excellent answer, Visal!

85
00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:00.904
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal verbs of possibility.

86
00:05:01.204 --> 00:05:05.331
Today's value is truthfulness. Tell the truth and check the facts.

87
00:05:05.631 --> 00:05:08.469
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

88
00:05:08.769 --> 00:05:11.433
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

